with q as (
  select $astro4_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "How do a solar and a lunar eclipse differ \u2014 which body is between which, whose shadow falls on whom, and at which phase?", "options": ["They are the same event seen from different places", "SOLAR: the Moon passes between Sun and Earth, casting the Moon's shadow on Earth \u2014 only at NEW Moon, and we see the Sun covered. LUNAR: the Earth passes between Sun and Moon, casting Earth's shadow on the Moon \u2014 only at FULL Moon, and we see the Moon darken to red", "Solar happens at full Moon and lunar at new Moon", "Both happen when the Moon's shadow falls on the Earth"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A solar eclipse needs the Moon on the sunward side of Earth \u2014 the new Moon \u2014 so it can slide in front of the Sun and drop its shadow on us. A lunar eclipse needs the Moon behind the Earth, opposite the Sun \u2014 the full Moon \u2014 so it can fall into Earth's shadow. Mirror images: at new Moon the Moon can block the Sun; at full Moon the Earth can block the sunlight reaching the Moon."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why is a total SOLAR eclipse visible only from a narrow track, while a LUNAR eclipse is seen from half the Earth?", "options": ["Solar eclipses are much rarer events than lunar ones", "The Moon's UMBRA makes only a small spot on Earth (~100\u2013270 km, the path of totality), so only observers on that thin ribbon see totality \u2014 whereas Earth's umbra at the Moon's distance is ~2.6\u00d7 the Moon's width, engulfing the whole Moon, so the darkened Moon is visible to everyone on the night side at once", "The Sun is brighter than the Moon", "Total solar eclipses only happen at the poles"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A shadow from an extended source has a dark umbra (total) and a partial penumbra. The Moon's umbra barely reaches Earth, painting a thin track \u2014 the path of totality \u2014 where alone the Sun is fully hidden; a huge surrounding penumbra sees only a partial bite. Earth's umbra, by contrast, easily swallows the whole Moon and is visible from the entire night hemisphere. So lunar eclipses are 'democratic' (half the planet sees each one) while a given spot sees a total solar eclipse only about once in ~375 years."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "There is a new Moon and a full Moon every month \u2014 so why isn't there a solar and a lunar eclipse every month?", "options": ["Because clouds usually block them", "Because the Moon's orbit is tilted about 5\u00b0 to the ecliptic, so most months the new/full Moon passes a little ABOVE or BELOW the exact Sun\u2013Earth line and its shadow misses; eclipses happen only when a new or full Moon lands near a NODE (where the tilted orbit crosses the ecliptic), so all three bodies truly line up", "Because the Moon is sometimes too far away to cast a shadow", "Because the Earth's shadow only reaches the Moon in winter"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The monthly new and full Moons align in the Sun's direction, but an eclipse also needs alignment in the perpendicular direction \u2014 the Moon exactly on the Sun\u2013Earth line, not above or below it. The 5\u00b0 orbital tilt means that usually fails: the shadow flies over or under its target into space. Only when the Moon is near a node \u2014 one of the two points where its tilted orbit crosses the ecliptic \u2014 are the three bodies genuinely collinear. Eclipses require the coincidence of the right phase AND proximity to a node, which is why they cluster a few times a year."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why is the Sun's yearly path called the 'ecliptic'?", "options": ["Because the Sun eclipses the stars along it", "Because it is the line on which eclipses occur: an eclipse requires the Moon to be ON the ecliptic (at a node, in that plane) AND aligned with the Sun (which always lies on the ecliptic) \u2014 so the Moon must reach the Sun's path, at a node, for the shadows to align", "Because the Moon travels exactly along it every night", "Because it marks the boundary of the zodiac"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The name, promised two chapters earlier, is paid off here. The Sun always sits on the ecliptic (it is the Sun's path); an eclipse needs the Moon to join it there \u2014 to arrive at a node, in the ecliptic plane, while new or full. Eclipses happen only when the Moon reaches the ecliptic at the right moment, so the Sun's path is quite literally the eclipse-line."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The Sun and Moon appear almost exactly the same size in our sky. Why, and what does that coincidence make possible?", "options": ["They really are the same size; that's why eclipses work", "The Sun is ~400\u00d7 LARGER than the Moon but, by chance, ~400\u00d7 FARTHER away, and since angular size = size \u00f7 distance the two 400s cancel \u2014 so both look ~0.5\u00b0 across. That near-match lets a total eclipse hide the blinding disk while baring the faint CORONA (and, with the Moon's varying distance, gives total vs annular eclipses)", "The Moon is much closer, so it looks bigger and always covers the Sun", "Atmospheric refraction magnifies the Moon to match the Sun"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Angular size is real diameter divided by distance. The Sun's ~400\u00d7 greater size is cancelled by its ~400\u00d7 greater distance, leaving it and the Moon each about half a degree across. Because the Moon is JUST big enough to cover the Sun's photosphere exactly, a total eclipse blocks the million-times-brighter disk while leaving the ghostly corona visible. The match isn't perfect \u2014 the Moon's elliptical orbit makes it slightly larger near perigee (total) or smaller near apogee (annular, a 'ring of fire') \u2014 nor permanent, since the receding Moon will one day be too small to cover the Sun."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Resolve the central puzzle of the chapter: why eclipses are rare despite monthly new and full Moons. Explain the 5\u00b0 tilt, the nodes, why the Sun's path is called the ecliptic, and how the node condition gives eclipses a predictable rhythm (eclipse seasons and the saros) \u2014 and why that predictability mattered so much for the history of science.", "rubric": "THE PUZZLE: there is a NEW Moon (Moon between us and Sun, positioned for a solar eclipse) and a FULL Moon (Moon behind Earth, positioned for a lunar eclipse) EVERY month \u2014 yet eclipses are rare (a few a year), not monthly. THE RESOLUTION \u2014 the 5\u00b0 TILT: the Moon's orbit is inclined ~5\u00b0 to the ECLIPTIC (the plane of Earth's orbit). An eclipse needs the Sun, Earth, and Moon VERY precisely collinear so the (fairly narrow) shadow hits its target. The monthly new/full Moon aligns in the Sun's DIRECTION (longitude), but because of the tilt it usually passes a little ABOVE or BELOW the exact Sun\u2013Earth line (the latitude alignment fails), so the shadow sails over the top or under the bottom of the target into empty space \u2014 no eclipse. THE NODES: the tilted orbit crosses the ecliptic plane at exactly TWO points, the NODES (ascending and descending); only there is the Moon exactly in the ecliptic, on the potential Sun\u2013Earth\u2013Moon line. So an eclipse requires a COINCIDENCE of two things: the right PHASE (new or full) AND the Moon being near a NODE. Both are regular; their coincidence is occasional. (New Moon near a node \u2192 solar eclipse; full Moon near a node \u2192 lunar eclipse.) WHY 'ECLIPTIC': the Sun's path is called the ecliptic because it is the LINE ON WHICH ECLIPSES OCCUR \u2014 the Moon must reach it (arrive at a node, in the ecliptic plane, aligned with the Sun, which always lies on it) for the shadows to align. THE RHYTHM \u2014 ECLIPSE SEASONS: an eclipse needs the Moon at a node WHEN the node line points toward/away from the Sun; as Earth orbits, the Sun's direction aligns with the node line TWICE a year (~6 months apart), opening two ~month-long ECLIPSE SEASONS, each usually holding a solar eclipse (its new Moon) and a lunar one (the full Moon ~2 weeks later) \u2014 hence at least ~4 eclipses/year. (The nodes slowly REGRESS over 18.6 years, so the seasons drift around the calendar \u2014 but regularly.) THE SAROS: after ~18 years 11 days the Sun\u2013Earth\u2013Moon geometry nearly repeats (a common multiple of the synodic, draconic, and anomalistic months), so a near-identical eclipse recurs \u2014 letting the BABYLONIANS predict eclipses from centuries of records. WHY IT MATTERED: eclipse prediction was one of the earliest, most powerful demonstrations that the heavens are LAWFUL, geometric, and PREDICTABLE \u2014 that even the sky's most dramatic, seemingly capricious event follows precise rules a human mind can compute (Thales's reputed 585 BC prediction). Turning terror into arithmetic proved the cosmos is ORDERED and COMPREHENSIBLE, not ruled by whim \u2014 a cornerstone of the scientific worldview. A strong answer ties it to the through-line: the apparent chaos of a darkening Sun is the shadow of a simple geometry (three bodies, one tilt, the nodes), and reading that geometry yields not just explanation but PREDICTION."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Explain the 'great coincidence' of eclipses in full \u2014 the size match and its cause, why it reveals the corona, the total-vs-annular distinction, and why it is a coincidence of TIME as well as size and distance. Then give two ways eclipses have served as scientific instruments.", "rubric": "THE SIZE MATCH: the Sun and Moon appear ALMOST EXACTLY THE SAME SIZE in the sky \u2014 each about half a degree (~0.5\u00b0; Sun ~32\u2032, Moon ~31\u2032). ITS CAUSE: the Sun is about 400\u00d7 LARGER than the Moon in diameter, but by pure chance also about 400\u00d7 FARTHER away; since ANGULAR SIZE = real size \u00f7 distance, the two factors of 400 CANCEL, leaving equal apparent sizes. WHY IT REVEALS THE CORONA: because the Moon is JUST big enough to cover the Sun's blinding disk (the photosphere) EXACTLY \u2014 not much bigger, not smaller \u2014 a total eclipse hides the overwhelming glare while leaving visible the Sun's faint outer atmosphere, the CORONA (a pearly halo, normally drowned out by a disk ~a million times brighter). If the Moon were much larger it would cover the corona too; if much smaller it couldn't block the disk. TOTAL vs ANNULAR: the match is close but NOT PERFECT because the Moon's orbit is slightly ELLIPTICAL, so its apparent size varies \u2014 near PERIGEE (closest) it looks slightly LARGER than the Sun and covers it fully \u2192 TOTAL (corona visible); near APOGEE (farthest) it looks slightly SMALLER and cannot cover it \u2192 ANNULAR, leaving a brilliant RING of Sun (the 'ring of fire', no corona). Which one occurs depends on where the Moon is in its orbit. A COINCIDENCE OF TIME: the match is not even PERMANENT \u2014 the Moon is slowly RECEDING (~3.8 cm/yr). In the deep PAST it was closer and appeared LARGER, so total eclipses were easy; in the far FUTURE it will be too small ever to cover the Sun, and total eclipses will CEASE, leaving only annular ones. We live in the particular cosmic WINDOW (a few hundred million years) when a small Moon just fits a vast Sun \u2014 and happen to have the eyes/minds to witness it. So it is a coincidence of SIZE, DISTANCE, and TIME. ECLIPSES AS INSTRUMENTS (any two): (1) the CORONA (and HELIUM) \u2014 the corona, invisible except during totality, was first studied during eclipses, and helium was first detected in the Sun's eclipse spectrum before being found on Earth (named for helios, the Sun); (2) SIZES/DISTANCES \u2014 lunar eclipses let the ancients (Aristarchus) measure the Moon's size from Earth's shadow and, with angular sizes, the Moon's and Sun's distances; (3) GENERAL RELATIVITY (1919) \u2014 during totality, with the Sun's glare blocked, astronomers photographed stars near the Sun's edge and measured how much its gravity BENT their light, confirming Einstein's prediction (possible only during an eclipse, when those stars are visible) and making him world-famous. COMMON THREAD: eclipses briefly HIDE the Sun or Moon, REVEALING what is normally hidden (the corona, stars near the Sun, the shadow's geometry) \u2014 nature's free scientific instrument. A strong answer conveys the WONDER (a small Moon exactly fitting a vast Sun to bare its crown of light, in this brief epoch that is ours) alongside the physics."}]$astro4_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/foundations/observed-sky/eclipses', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
